+## Installing it
+
+Downloads are on the [PixelSmith releases page](https://github.com/authorss81/pixelsmith/releases).
+Two shapes, and the difference matters:
+
+| Platform | What you get | How to run it |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Windows** | `pixelsmith-<version>-windows-x64.zip` | Unzip anywhere — the desktop, a USB stick — and run `shrinkray.exe`. No installer, no admin rights, no registry writes. |
+| **Android** | `pixelsmith-<version>-android.apk`, and `.aab` for Play | Sideload the APK (Settings → allow installing from your browser), or install from the Play listing. |
+
+**Windows will warn you, and it is not a mistake.** The EXE is not code-signed,
+because a signing certificate is issued to an organisation and paid for by one,
+and this project has neither. SmartScreen calls an unsigned binary an
+"unrecognised app", shows a blue dialog, and — on some builds — refuses to run
+it until you click **More info → Run anyway**. Every other unsigned program gets
+the same warning. If you would rather not click through a warning about a
+program you have not read, build it yourself: it is four commands in
+[Building](#building) and the source is the whole repository.
+
+The alternative — an MSIX or an MSI — would be signed too, or would prompt for
+admin rights, or both. A portable ZIP is the honest shape for an unsigned
+project, and the README says so rather than hoping nobody notices.
+
+**Which checksum to compare.** Every release attaches a `SHA256SUMS.txt`. After
+downloading:
+
+```bash
+sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt
+```
+
+And the sizes: a Windows bundle is roughly 30 MB and an APK roughly 60 MB. Both
+are larger than a photo resizer "should" be, because the Rust engine is statically
+linked into each binary and the APK carries four ABIs' worth of it. That is the
+cost of shipping no dynamic dependencies and no installer.
+
